# Store screenshots

Both store listings are built by GitHub workflows and committed back to this
repo. The copy lives in `headlines.json`, in one file, because two different
tools read it — so the listings cannot end up saying different things.

## The two pipelines

They are deliberately not the same tool, because no tool does both.

| | Play | App Store |
|---|---|---|
| Workflow | **Screenshots (Android)** | **Screenshots (iOS)** |
| Runner | `ubuntu-latest` | `macos-latest` |
| Capture | `integration_test` on an emulator | goldie, replaying argent flows |
| Framing | headline composited over the raw capture | device bezel, background, headline |
| Copy from | `headlines.json` | `headlines.json`, via `goldie.config.ts` |

[goldie](https://github.com/kacperkapusciak/goldie) is iOS-only by
construction — its whole device layer is `xcrun simctl` and its device list is
one entry, iPhone 17 Pro Max. It cannot produce Play assets, which is why
Android composites its own.

## Changing the copy

Edit `headlines.json`. One entry per scene:

```json
{ "id": "home", "headline": { "en-US": "Everything in one place" } }
```

Scene ids match the capture names the integration test writes **and** the
argent flow names goldie replays. Add a screen and all three move together.

These are store copy, not UI strings: write the benefit, not the feature name.
The `app-store-optimization` skill covers what belongs here — and note that
most people never scroll past the first two.
{{#ios}}

## Before the iOS workflow can run

goldie replays **argent flows**, and they do not exist until someone records
them. This is a Mac-side job, once:

```sh
npm i -g goldie @swmansion/argent
```

Record one flow per scene in `headlines.json`, named `store-<id>` — so
`store-home`, `store-settings`, and so on. They land in `.argent/flows` and are
committed. `goldie doctor` checks the toolchain and the flows before a real
run.

Until the flows are committed the iOS workflow fails at its first step, with a
message saying so. That is deliberate: a green run that produced no images is
the worse outcome.

Flows break when the UI changes. Re-record them, or ask an agent to repair
them — goldie ships its own skill for that, installed per machine rather than
vendored here:

```
/plugin marketplace add kacperkapusciak/goldie
/plugin install goldie@goldie
```

**No preview video yet.** Apple takes a 15-30 second recording, which needs its
own flows; `goldie.config.ts` has no `preview` scene until you add them.
{{/ios}}

## Where the output goes

- Play — `android/fastlane/metadata/android/<locale>/images/phoneScreenshots/`
{{#ios}}
- App Store — `ios/fastlane/screenshots/<locale>/`
{{/ios}}

Both are the directories `supply`{{#ios}} and `deliver`{{/ios}} upload from, so
a committed screenshot is already in the release path — there is no separate
assembly step at release time.

## Languages

{{#hasNepali}}The Play listing is bilingual; the App Store one is English only.
That is Apple's constraint, not an oversight — App Store Connect accepts a
fixed list of languages that includes `hi`, `bn-BD` and `ur-PK` but **no
Nepali**. {{/hasNepali}}`headlines.json` carries every locale either store can
use; each pipeline takes the ones its store accepts.
